\chapter{Elaboration, semantic checking, and grounded HIR}
\label{ch:73}
\index{Elaboration, semantic checking, and grounded HIR}

Elaboration solves type relationships, specializes admitted polymorphic functions, assigns checked identities and produces a grounded program. Semantic checking verifies concrete types, local updates, call signatures, return paths and scoped resource use. The frontend integrates module resolution, hygienic expansion, PPX and quotation checks before the final checked representation is consumed. A transform that changes a call must preserve its resolved target and re-establish the relevant obligations.

\section{Diagnose by phase}
For an ambiguous source annotation, first inspect the constraints implied by its call sites. For a call that resolves to the wrong function, inspect binding identities through module and macro processing. For a concrete HIR parameter with a forged nominal shape, test the independent checker rather than the parser. This triage narrows regressions while respecting the distinction between inferring an unknown, checking a known type, and approving a concrete layout for an emitter. \cref{ch:78} follows the next boundary into canonical values.

\section{Solve a recursive signature on paper}
The inference fixture contains this definition and a call with the
argument five:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn factorial(n) -> _ {
  if n == 0 { return 1; }
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Assign names to the two unknowns: the type of the parameter and the
type returned by the recursive call. The call site supplies an integer.
The subtraction must yield an argument suitable for another invocation
of the same function. The base return supplies an integer result; the
recursive return must produce that same type. Nothing requires running
the recursion to find its signature. Once checked, executing the five
recursive multiplication steps down to the base case produces 120. That
execution checks the function's meaning, an additional observation beyond
the successful solution of its type constraints.

Contrast three possible failures. An unused function that merely prints an
unannotated argument provides too little information to choose a concrete
printable type. A parameter applied to itself, as in \OnxCodeTerm{f(f)},
requires its type to contain itself; this is an infinite-type constraint,
not a request for one more call site. Finally, a function that returns one
on its true branch and falls through on its false branch can have a clear
integer result obligation and still lack a return on all paths. Adding an
integer result annotation addresses none of the missing control flow.
Use the distinction to decide whether to supply evidence, change the
expression, or repair the branch. In particular, testing only the call
with a true flag would conceal the missing return that the checker must
consider for a false flag.

